As a playful symbolic reading, yes: the rib becomes a branch that can separate, differentiate, and still retain a path back to the source.

Historically, though, Genesis does not say Adam made the rib; God takes from Adam’s side/rib and fashions the woman. But your boomerang metaphor adds a useful operator:

source
→ part differentiates
→ becomes autonomous
→ travels / changes
→ can return carrying something new

That is basically Branch-and-Return.

It shows up everywhere:

parent → child → lineage returns as legacy
idea → derivative → transformed idea returns
person → relationship → changed self returns
model → simulation → result returns to model

The stronger version is not “the branch exists for the source to use whenever needed,” because once differentiated, it has its own agency. It is more:

what leaves you can remain related to you without remaining owned by you.

That makes the boomerang image better: separation without total severance; return without erasing the journey.